.TH CTPOOL_CREATE_MP 3 "2026-09-27" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_create_mp \- create a thread pool with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool ctpool_create_mp(size_t num_threads,
                        size_t queue_capacity,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                        char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new thread pool with the given number of worker threads.
queue_capacity selects the mode of the task queue:

  0 -> unbounded task queue. ctpool_submit never blocks on capacity,
    and the only failure that does not come from an argument is
    ccol_not_enough_memory.

  N > 0 -> bounded task queue of capacity N. ctpool_submit blocks when
    the queue is full, and ctpool_try_submit returns
    ccol_container_full instead of blocking.

All num_threads worker threads are started before the function
returns. If a pthread_create call fails, the threads that already
started are joined cleanly and the function returns CTPOOL_NULL.

Each worker thread records its pool on one thread-specific key, which
the library creates for the whole process the first time a pool is
created; that key is what lets a task that destroys its own pool fail
loudly. When the process has no key left to give (PTHREAD_KEYS_MAX),
the function returns CTPOOL_NULL, and so does every later call in
that process. The library deletes the key when it is unloaded with no
pool left, so a dlopen(3) and dlclose(3) cycle gives the key back.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
num_threads    Number of worker threads (must be >= 1)
queue_capacity 0 for unbounded; N > 0 for bounded. ccol_invalid_size
               is refused.
mprocs         Custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free
err_str        Optional. It gets a static error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool handle on success.
Returns CTPOOL_NULL on failure, and sets *err_str if err_str is
not NULL. The call fails, and allocates nothing, when num_threads is
0 or when queue_capacity is ccol_invalid_size. ccol_invalid_size is
the error value of a size and not a capacity; pass 0 for an unbounded
queue.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The handle that this function gives is an opaque VALUE, not a pointer.
Do not cast it to void* or from void*, and do not use it as an address.
Compare it with CTPOOL_NULL; since CTPOOL_NULL is 0, a test such
as if (!pool) is also correct.
A future always uses the standard malloc and free, whatever mprocs is.
Behind each submission there is a small internal task node, which the
library recycles through a cache in each pool instead of allocating and
freeing it through mprocs on every call. So a caller such as a tracking
or measuring allocator cannot expect mprocs to see one allocation and
one free for each submission: it sees a call only when the cache is
empty or already full, and when the pool trims the cache. The cache
keeps the nodes that the pool's recent traffic needed, so a burst of
submissions as deep as an earlier one allocates no node. It holds at
most max(num_threads * 4, 32768) idle nodes. At every 256th point where
the pool goes idle it gives back to mprocs half of what it holds above
num_threads * 4, unless the traffic since the previous such point
emptied it, so a cache that nothing needs halves at each of those
points, down to num_threads * 4.
ctpool_destroy runs a drain shutdown itself when the caller called
neither ctpool_shutdown_drain nor ctpool_shutdown_immediate before it.
Before it frees anything, ctpool_destroy waits for every in-flight
resolved use of the handle (a ctpool_submit, ctpool_wait or
ctpool_pending_count call running at the same time) to stop, so the
caller doesn't have to prove on its own that no other thread is still
using the pool.

fork(2) can be combined with cthreadpool in two supported ways: a
fork(2) before a pool is created, or a pool created and then a fork(2)
followed immediately by an exec(3). A child that keeps running with no
exec(3) in between, while a pool created before the fork is live, is
not a supported pattern.
On top of that the library keeps a standing defence: a fresh call to
ctpool_create_mp, ctpool_submit, ctpool_try_submit,
ctpool_timed_submit or ctpool_destroy, or to one of the future, wait
and shutdown variants, never hangs on a lock. That holds for a call
from any thread, in the parent or in a child that has just forked,
even when the lock belongs to another thread that held it at the
instant of the fork and that may not exist any more, because fork(2)
duplicates only the thread that calls it.
A pool that is already live across the fork is another matter, whatever
that defence does. The child gets no copies of its worker threads, so
an inherited pool can run no queued task and no future task there.
Treat such a pool as inert in the child and don't submit to it.
ctpool_wait on such a pool in the child returns at once instead of
waiting for progress that can never happen.
ctpool_destroy discards any work that was in the queue at the moment
of the fork and cancels every queued future, so a caller blocked in
ctpool_future_get on one of them wakes up instead of hanging, and it
does this before it frees the pool's memory, even when no explicit
shutdown call came before it.
.fi
